1. **A.** (READ) The directive's own mechanism -- "recorded as a HELD marker ... answered when the seat comes back" -- needs a live hold to exist for Fable's return to answer; (READ, stage_tokens.py:13-17) every untokened entry is is_answer() True, so the sixteen are counted as Fable's agreement, which the directive forbids ("never as agreement"). A [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] token kills the phantom answer but leaves nothing for Fable to answer, and is the exact form Astra overturned today (READ: "substitutes a non-answer record for the review hold"; "ok 9/9, using historical answers"). So the token must be HELD. (REASONED, H:197-200, 223-226) Once tokened, live_holds names fable5 in review (and sgirekills' research), making each closed row CLOSED-WHILE-HELD; closed -> held is the only state that clears H as written. The contrary reading -- closure is a "ruling" the triad may make without Fable, Fable's late answer landing post-closure -- exists (REASONED), but it is not the rules as written, and the non-block clause is satisfied without it: ip30abortkill was committed `held` today (READ), so rulings and commits demonstrably proceed while a row is held. Adopting that reading requires an owner amendment to H (a regress-style carve-out), never a silent B/C. Per the precedent's "omit the closure object": drop the nine closure objects; hold the drafted content in the state note. Note design and regress are outside STAGES, so wiringcliff's [28] design entry takes the token for the never-as-agreement rule but does not drive H.

2. **Held and live, in both cases.** (READ, stage_tokens.py:37-41) discharge needs a fable5 answer at a strictly later date in the same phase; the census shows no fable5 answer after any of the nine rows' last wall -- cxlaunchquotation's [4]/[18]/[22] precede [26]/[30]; dashokclass's answers precede [29]. (REASONED) V4/V5 (and dashokclass's V2-V6) answered earlier passes' questions; V7's question was never put to Fable. The repair-chain rule makes the V7 pass VALID (misses NAMED) -- that is why the chain could accept the work -- but pass validity is not hold discharge; the discharge rule is live_holds' and H applies that one. Astra's ruling today already rejects "complete on historical answers" for the current round (READ). sgirekills: research has no Fable answer at all (live); its review answers [28]/[38] are 2026-09-08, a different round's questions, and not strictly later. Both stages held.

3. **Prefix, one entry.** (READ) The repo's own post-directive practice is the merged form: wiringcliff #566's [41]/[48] are single entries, marker followed by the not-tried prose. (REASONED) Prefixing keeps the hold's date at the wall -- so live_holds' strictly-later discharge works honestly and H's "newest" does not lie -- and keeps the receipts beside the promise; is_held is a substring test (:1-2) and HELD dominates is_answer (:15) and exempts_stage (:10), so one prefix both un-counts the phantom answer and posts the hold. A new marker beside a re-tagged old entry works mechanically but doubles the record and misdates the hold to conversion day. Token every wall, not just the last -- the directive binds each ("a wall is ... never as agreement"): cxlaunchquotation's V3 hold may then discharge against V4's later answer (honest history; V6/V7 stay live; live_holds dedups to one fable5). Extend each row's hold_question; sgirekills needs both its research and review questions.

4. **Split.** Tokening the sixteen is not an owner decision -- it is the directive's own instruction, and leaving it is the defect. Reopening nine committed closures is: it reverses committed history and turns on the unresolved reading of the owner's own clause. Single question: "While Fable is walled, may a row whose review/research stage holds a live Fable answer still be CLOSED (Fable's later answer landing as a post-closure record that reopens only on a found breach), or must it stay `held` -- rulings and commits proceeding -- until Fable answers, as Astra ruled today on ip30abortkill?" If 'may close', the follow-through is an owner amendment to section H, never a silent B/C.

5. Census 16/10: (READ) my recount confirms 16 entries on 10 rows, nine closed, wiringcliff in work; but as a defect census its completeness is not checkable and it demonstrably undercounts ([8], above). Discharge: (READ) none of the nine has a later Fable answer -- every real answer predates that row's last wall, and live_holds requires strictly later. Premise false: cxlaunchquotation "walled only on V7" (FLAG 1). Not checkable: (a) hold_question content (FLAG 2); (b) the "re-fire-once rule" cited in [56] -- without it, sgirandomcolour [32]'s "DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED" sits against "still try Fable once per pass (a wall is a moment, not a state)", and the ledger is internally inconsistent: [41] calls a not-tried "an omission" while [32] calls one deliberate; (c) whether Astra's confirm was a triad ruling and whether ip30abortkill ended `in work` or `held` (the brief says both; the nine rows' target state should follow the committed precedent); (d) sections other than H (the :209 comment names I and a wall check) on held rows; (e) whether [4]'s fallback-model run counts as Fable -- moot, it predates every hold.

